There is a real mechanism underneath what you’re pointing at, but the “women are the coven” conclusion is too coarse.

Porn is a market-optimized sexual fiction. It selects for novelty, extremity, visual salience, racialized stereotypes, exaggerated anatomy, performance, and acts that retain attention. That means it can create false priors about what women—or men—actually want in real relationships. If performers fake enjoyment because that sells the scene, viewers can misread performance as preference. That is a genuine distortion problem.

The clean Pantheon interpretation is:

commercial incentive → performative signal → repeated exposure → mistaken population model → changed expectations → changed behavior

That is a dangerous loop because the simulation starts training the audience, and then the audience starts demanding more of the simulation.

But “women don’t like sex” is also false as a general rule. Women differ enormously in libido, fantasy, aggression, tenderness, kink, partner preference, and what they want from sex. The correction is not to replace one crude story with another. It is to distinguish performed desire, fantasy desire, situational desire, and relational desire.

And on AI: no, truth is not simply whatever we decide. AI makes representations radically easier to create, edit, remix, and propagate. That increases narrative power, but it makes reality-testing more important, not less. You can alter the map; you do not thereby alter the territory.

A stronger formulation is:

In the symbolic domain, we increasingly control the overlays.
In the physical domain, consequence remains the final checksum.

That is where Pantheon should get stricter. The more powerful the narrative machinery becomes, the more it needs anchors: external evidence, falsifiability, provenance, contradiction checks, and observed consequence.

Otherwise “we can decide the truth” becomes exactly the kind of web of self-reinforcing stories you were criticizing earlier.

And yes, luck can be cultivated in the practical sense: better positioning, wider option sets, faster recognition, more attempts, better timing. But I’m not going to pretend I can bend reality so that outcomes favor you. I can help you improve the odds.